Date: Sat, 25 Sep 93 20:23:02 -0700
From: mcpherso@lumina.ucsd.edu (John McPherson)
Subject: SPEECH: Extropianism 101

  Below is a first draft of a speech on Extropianism that I will be giving
in 3 weeks to my toastmaster club.  The members tend to favor libertarianism
and self-improvement.  My intention is to give a down-to-earth description of
the topic with popular appeal, so I didn't get technical and I didn't mention
the host of intricacies discussed on this list, but I do hope to show that 
this is a serious endeavor and that people are working on these ideas in 
earnest.
  Please let me know how the piece strikes you, and how you think it might 
strike the rank uninitiated.  Did I inadvertantly leave out anything of
major importance?  Are there any concepts that I bungled or could have said
more elegantly?  If you have any ideas on selling Extropianism I'd be 
interested in them.
  When I hammer out the final version of the speech, I'll post it to the
Exi-essay list.

Flames to /dev/nul
Constructive comments to: Extropians@extropy.org or mcpherso@lumina.ucsd.edu

    --------================$$$$ Ex101 $$$$================--------

                        "Extropianism 101"
                                by
                          John McPherson

  "Forward! Upward! Outward! Into the Future!"  This is the rallying cry of
the Extropian futurist movement.  "Who are these Extropians?", you may ask.
First, I must say that Extropians are among the most fiercely individualistic
people I know of, so my whole speech is to be taken only as a general 
description which may or may not apply to any particular person!  That having
been said, basically, Extropians are people interested in living greatly 
extended lives in a greatly technology-enhanced lifestyle, perhaps even in 
space, and they agree that some form of libertarianism is the best socio-
politico-economic system to encourage and maintain that state of affairs.  
Extropians also want to improve and upgrade themselves, perhaps to a degree 
that may stun even the most open-minded people, for you see, many Extropians 
want to become immortal ... more on that later!

  Let's take a look at the word "extropy" ... what in tarnation does _that_
mean?  _Ex_tropy is the opposite of _en_tropy, and many of you probably
know that "entropy" refers to that tendency of the universe which promotes
degradation, disorder, erosion and death, so, "extropy" refers to that
opposite tendency which promotes improvement, building-up of energy, 
vitality and intelligence, progressive self-organization and upgrading ...
in other words, the very characteristics of life itself at its best.
Anything supportive of this positive tendency may well be "extropic" in
nature.

  "Do you want to live forever?"  Many Extropians would answer a resounding
"Yes!"  Incredulously, you might ask "How?!"  Simple.  They have mapped out
a 4-step process: life extension, cryonics, nanotechnology, and uploading.
Here's the plan:

  First, you take care of yourself as best you can, and increase your chances
of living as long as you can, perhaps by following the dietary advise of
Dr. Roy Walford who wrote "The 120-Year Diet" [show book to audience].

  Second, if you don't manage to live long enough to gain access to the
immortalist breakthrough, you can have your recently-deanimated body deep
frozen by Alcor Life Extension Foundation [show booklet to audience], where
it will wait essentially unchanged in cryonic suspension until technology 
allows you to be resusitated.

  Third, within 100 years we'll probably have micro-miniaturized technology,
or "nanotechnology", developed to the point where molecular-sized robots
can repair damage to your body at the cellular level, as described by Eric
Drexler [show "Unbounding the Future" to audience].  Nanotechnology will 
probably be the way that people are revived from their cryonic suspension.

  Fourth, for the long haul, many will probably opt for replacing various
parts of their body or brain with more durable and efficient mechanisms ...
perhaps even uploading themselves into super-advanced robots with lightning-
fast massively-parallel embedded computers, as described by Hans Moravec
[show "Mind Children" to audience].  This, of course, is something for
the deep future!

  If any of this sounds "way out there", consider how fast technology has
been advancing this century.  It is increasing at an accelerated pace!
Extropians are merely anticipating and planning for what already seems
quite feasible even now to humanity's best minds.

  What are the driving forces behind developments such as these?
Unabashed optimism, the assuming of personal responsibility, diligently
applied and expanded creative intelligence, and the socio-economic freedom
to experiment and act.  Extropians want to live in a libertarian free society
where they can pursue these projects in peace, enjoy their lives in whatever
peacable ways they desire, and create wealth for themselves and others to as
great an extent as they aspire to.  Extropians want to maximize their 
intelligence through mind-enhancing technologies such as the recently
discovered nootropic drugs described by Dr. Ward Dean [show "Smart Drugs and
Nutrients" to the audience], through the computer-based technologies of
virtual reality, hypermedia, artificial intelligence and neural networks,
and through the future technology of direct brain-to-computer linkages.

  Let me describe a few of these in greater depth, namely: smart drugs,
hypermedia and artificial intelligence.

  Psychopharmacologists have discovered and developed a variety of drugs
which can temporarily expand your intelligence and range of experience.
Hydergine has been shown to enhance cognition in the elderly.  Piracetam
has been shown to enhance memory and the flow of information between the
cerebral hemispheres.  Vasopressin improves concentration and the imprinting
of new information in your brain.  Lucidril boosts intelligence and removes
age deposits from the brain!  Other drugs of interest to some Extropians are:
cannabis for its relaxing properties, LSD for its ability to expand and
illuminate one's consciousness, and MDMA for its experience of sheer mammalian
warmth and empathy.  Unfortunately these last 3 are currently illegal, and
have some distracting side effects.  An ideal drug, which I'll dub "Extropine",
would have the relaxation of cannabis without loss of short-term memory, the
consciousness expansion of LSD without the loss of orientation to reality,
the warmth and comfort of MDMA without getting carried away by it, the
concentration enhancement of vasopressin, and the improvement in memory and
cognition offered by Lucidril, Piracetam and Hydergine.  "Extropine" would
be quite an interesting drug indeed!

  Hypermedia [show book on H-text to audience] is a way of structuring
information in an expandable network of reference links that _you_ can make
to suit your own interests, as opposed to the linear one-dimensional methods
currently used by almost all forms of communication, such as books,
newspapers, radio and TV.  With a hypermedia system, you can go off in 
wholly new directions just by clicking a button, you can create new links
and lines of study just as easily, and you can retrace your steps in case
you get too excited and wind up someplace very far from your original question!

  Artifical intelligence will enable us to function at a much higher level
by storing patterns of knowledge and by offloading certain tasks that 
machines can handle.  Expert systems can guide us through complicated tasks
that we know next to nothing about, because they will store the insights and
rules of thumb used by people who _are_ experts in such things.  "Knowbots"
can assist us in research by autonomously searching out information in vast
library archives just based on general things we "tell" them to look for.  
Fuzzy systems and neural networks seem to be able to "learn" simple tasks 
the same way that we do!

  After hearing all these things Extropians are interested in, you might
ask "Why?"  Why would someone want to live for a thousand years, or augment
their intelligence, or upgrade their body?   My current answer is: knowledge,
experience and glory ... to know, to see and to do.  With enough time and
ruggedness, you can see the whole world!  Heck, you can see the universe!
You can learn whole new fields that you never would've gotten around to
otherwise.  You can read all those books you've been meaning to get to, and
more!  You can work on ever bigger and grander projects, maybe even someday
constructing on the stellar level!  You can enjoy the experience of accumulated
successes, innovations and breakthroughs that before might've only been
once-in-a-lifetime experiences.  You can share the exquisite pleasure of
passing on quality knowledge and of mentoring younger people who are only
2 or 3 hundred years old!

  In conclusion, Extropians want to live as long as possible in a maximally
free society, and to grow in knowledge, creativity, intelligence, and ability,
to the fullest possible extent.  Personally, I applaud their efforts and 
look forward to the day I can call myself an Extropian!

  Mr. Toastmaster!

    --------================$$$$ Ex101 $$$$================--------

John McPherson
Student of Extropianism


