> James Donald's wondering what measurements Einstein took).  I refer you 
> to Webster's definition of operationalism, which nowhere says a practioner
> actually has to have their head stuck up a spectroscope.  

Webster correctly defines operationalism as the view that
experimental operations must define scientific terms and
concepts, which is close to the definitions that I am used
to. Thus operationalism (so defined) is trivially false. 
Experiments test scientific terms and concepts, they do not
define them. Scientific terms and concepts must have
meaningful implications for experiments, but this is not
the same thing as being the outcome of a class of
experiment.

It is obviously sensible to define some things, for example
charge, momentum and inertia, operationally.  To define the
spin of an electron operationally is rather strained.

Similarly no one doubts that a flux line in a
superconductor is a real thing, yet to define one
operationally is absurd.  An operationalist must wind up
saying that many things that are plainly real, are either
meaningless or not real, and he will therefore end up in
very deep water philosophically.

Indeed the relationship is quite the other way around,
since concepts allow us to say that one experiment is like
another, and another experiment different, and thus decide
what constitutes replication of an experiment.

Thus concepts define experiments.  Experiments do not
define concepts.

Obviously, before Newton, the Copernican world view could
not be defined in terms of operations.  Similarly you
cannot define a ket in terms of operations.

To define renormalization in terms of operations is absurd.

In General Relativity space-time is well defined
operationally, but the reasoning that lead Einstein and
others to adopt general relativity is meaningless in terms
of operationalism.

