Subject: Meaninglessness

What Price means by operationalism seems to undergo
continual, drastic, and frequent shifts, according to the
momentary advantage in a particular argument, (and is
therefore meaningless)

One moment his definition is narrow, to exclude Starr, and
just about everybody in the universe, the next it is broad,
so as to include the scientific method and all the greats
of science, and also include every sane person in the
universe.

But in a posting elsewhere he committed himself to the
nicely simplistic definition given in Webster's dictionary,
which forms a beautiful stick with which to beat him.

Webster's definition:  The view that experimental
operations must define scientific terms and concepts.

By this definition then Newton's laws of motion and
Einstein's theory of General Relativity are certainly
operationalist theories, but Newton and Einstein were
certainly not operationalists, and the Copernican cosmology
was certainly not an operationalist theory, nor is modern
biology and geology.  Thus according the nicely simplistic
definition used in Webster's, most of modern science is
meaningless and unscientific, the cosmology of Copernicus,
Kepler, and Galileo was meaningless and unscientific,
though Galileo's laws of motion were meaningful and
scientific, and though the theories of Newton and Einstein
were meaningful and scientific, Newton was unscientific in
coming to his theory by unscientific reasoning, and
Einstein and the early adopters of General Relativity were
unscientific in developing the theory and accepting it for
unscientific and meaningless reasons.

Furthermore you cannot give an operational definition of a
dog.  Do dogs therefore not exist?  Is the concept of dog
unscientific?

In <17492@price.demon.co.uk>, price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)  wrote:

> Newton was quite clear that we all we know of
> objects is through their interactions.  I quote the Great Man:
> "... the qualities of bodies are only known to us by
> experiments ... we are certainly not to relinquish the
> evidence of experiments for the sake of dreams and vain
> fictions of our own devising..."

If experiments are evidence, then they are evidence of
something.  If scientific terms are *defined* by
experiments, you do not need evidence of something, just
the experiments themselves, a logically incoherent and
absurd position.

Price says:

> And here is a sample from the Hooke-Newton flamewar:    
> "You know the proper Method for inquiring after the
> properties of things is to deduce them from
> experiments."

This simply is not operationalism.  If you are an
operationalist you do not *deduce* the properties of things
from experiments.  The properties of things *are* what
things do in experiments.

These quotes are simple proof that Newton was not an
operationalist, at least not an operationalist according to
the simplistic definition given in Webster's.

I am sure that if Price puts his mind to it he can invent
or discover a philosophical category that includes most
great scientists and excludes Starr , (Trouble is of course
there were quite a lot of great scientists, and most of
them were naive realists or moderate realists, and since
Objectivism is pretty close to being moderate realism, this
is not as easy as you might expect).  But such a category
will have little resemblance to operationalism, and might
well also exclude Price.

