Phil Fraering:
> I expect the lack of contract reliability will simply be
> reflected in the current insurance market through much higher
> prices, 

That it will, but the problem is that insurance rates lump
a bunch of information into one package: the risk of
natural disaster, the risk of legal problems relating to
contracts, etc.  Catastrophe futures can abstract out the separate 
risks: one market for natural disasters, one for legal 
problems, one for moral hazard, etc., where companies can hedge 
and domain experts can speculate in each market.  My question/challenge 
is to rigorously define legal catastrophe futures; eg ones that would
have anticipated the recent breakdown of the U.S. tort system
that has jeopardized Lloyd's.

By quantifying and spreading risk more widely,
catastrophe futures might also make possible new kinds
of products, eg insurance against damage by war, terrorism,
riot, confiscatory taxation, forfeiture, criminal penalties,
etc. none of which can currently be insured against (that I'm aware of) 
because of uncertain statistical profile, very high potential damage 
claims, illegality in some jurisdictions, and in some cases moral 
hazard.  Tax insurance in particular could become a major driving force 
of crypto-anarchy.

